<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the app and Electron's helpers when the release is signed with
  Psyche's own self-signed certificate (docs/design/updates-and-install-2026.md §2.3, docs/signing.md).
  The same as entitlements.plist plus ONE key:

  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: a self-signed certificate has no team ID.
    With no team ID the hardened runtime refuses to load any library that Apple did not sign, even
    one signed with the same certificate ("mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have
    different Team IDs"), so Electron cannot load Electron Framework.framework at all without it.
    Measured on macOS 27 with a certificate made by deploy/macos/make-signing-cert.sh.

  What it costs, plainly: with library validation off, malware running as the same user that can
  write inside a user-owned /Applications/Psyche.app can swap a dylib inside the bundle. The swapped
  library then runs inside this process and inherits Psyche's TCC grants (microphone, screen, Apple
  Events, Contacts, Calendar) and its keychain entries. The hardened runtime stays on, so DYLD_*
  injection, debugger attach and task_for_pid stay blocked, but none of that stops a swapped dylib. The
  Electron fuses cover only Electron processes (this one and its helpers). The app also checks its own
  bundle seal once a day and turns its TCC-backed features off when that fails (bundle-integrity.ts).
  That check is a tripwire for an attacker who does not know it is there, not containment: a swapped
  library that loads at launch runs before the check and can hide it. The Gateway keeps running. A
  Developer ID build does not use this file.

  PsycheHelper.app does not have this key: it links only system libraries.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
